WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.260
In molecular biology,

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.450
most experiments include a step where we want to see the molecules that we're working with.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.190
For example,

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:13.080
suppose we're testing whether an enzyme can cut DNA at a specific sequence 

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:13.690
location.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:18.870
We might start by mixing the enzyme with some DNA of known sequence.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.370
But how do we know if anything happened? Did the enzyme cut at the expected 

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:25.030
location?

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.510
Did the reaction go to completion?

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:30.390
Typically,

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.050
we would use gel electrophoresis to find out.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.520
Gel electrophoresis is one of the best and most widely used techniques for characterizing 

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:39.510
biomolecules.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.030
A gel is a porous molecular sieve,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.030
and it can be used to separate mixtures of molecules and spread them out over a large area 

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.150
for visualization.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.140
For this segment,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.860
we're going to focus on separation of DNA with agarose gels.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.350
But bear in mind, that other molecules,

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.270
like RNA and proteins,

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.780
can be separated with other types of gels.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.880
Let's see what happens when we perform electrophoresis on this sample.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:11.450
We can use a pipette to transfer a small amount of the sample into a well in the gel.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.670
Once the sample is loaded,

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.470
we can connect the gel apparatus to a power source and apply a current.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.720
The resulting electric field exerts a force on the negatively charged molecules,

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:24.810
and they migrate towards the positive terminal.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:28.380
Meanwhile,

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.200
the gel acts as a sieve.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.650
Smaller molecules tend to move more quickly through the gel,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.230
while larger molecules move slowly. After an hour or two, 

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.250
the individual species will separate according to how fast they move,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.980
and when enough of the molecules migrate at the same speed,

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.950
they can be visualized as bands.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:49.460
Typically,

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.550
each band is comprised of billions of individual molecules.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.950
Once the gel has run for a sufficient amount of time,

00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:00.430
the next step is typically to scan the gel slab and acquire a digital image.

00:02:02.523 --> 00:02:04.853
Now that we have an idea of what the gel does,

00:02:05.173 --> 00:02:10.143
let's revisit our original questions and see how we can design our experiment to get more meaningful 

00:02:10.143 --> 00:02:10.593
data.

00:02:11.383 --> 00:02:11.943
First,

00:02:12.073 --> 00:02:16.163
we almost always include a reference sample that will produce bands of known sizes.

00:02:16.883 --> 00:02:18.023
This is called a ladder.

00:02:18.613 --> 00:02:20.813
If we scan our gel and see the ladder,

00:02:20.823 --> 00:02:22.363
we know that the gel is working,

00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:25.783
and we can tell exactly how fast certain band sizes are running.

00:02:26.683 --> 00:02:27.543
On the other hand,

00:02:27.893 --> 00:02:30.263
if the ladder bands are distorted or missing,

00:02:30.393 --> 00:02:33.553
we already have a hint that something might be wrong with our gel preparation,

00:02:33.783 --> 00:02:38.623
or perhaps with the ladder itself. Thinking about the question of how our 

00:02:38.623 --> 00:02:39.953
enzyme cuts the DNA,

00:02:40.103 --> 00:02:45.033
we may want to include another sample in our experiment that shows us what happens if the enzyme 

00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:46.693
doesn't cut the DNA at all.

00:02:47.783 --> 00:02:52.463
One approach would be to prepare a sample that is nearly identical to the first sample,

00:02:52.653 --> 00:02:56.333
except we would replace the enzyme with an equal volume of water.

00:02:57.183 --> 00:03:02.133
This control sample should give us a precise reference band for any uncut DNA in our 

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:05.333
experiment, and serve as a sanity check that we,

00:03:05.333 --> 00:03:05.723
in fact,

00:03:05.723 --> 00:03:07.893
started with the correct DNA as input.

00:03:08.983 --> 00:03:09.583
Finally,

00:03:09.593 --> 00:03:12.053
the full reaction sample would go in our third lane.

00:03:12.843 --> 00:03:13.083
Now,

00:03:13.083 --> 00:03:14.103
when we run the gel,

00:03:14.113 --> 00:03:15.523
we get a lot of information.

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:19.003
We see that the ladder bands are running at the expected sizes,

00:03:19.683 --> 00:03:23.053
we see that the uncut DNA is also the expected size,

00:03:23.633 --> 00:03:28.493
and we see that the two smaller bands correspond to two pieces of DNA that 

00:03:28.493 --> 00:03:31.373
resulted from a single cut at the expected site.

00:03:32.383 --> 00:03:34.493
If there's a trace amount of uncut DNA,

00:03:34.503 --> 00:03:38.943
we could quantify the relative amount to get an idea of the efficiency of our reaction.

00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:44.203
All of this planning also made us better prepared to notice unexpected results,

00:03:44.213 --> 00:03:46.423
which could be the harbingers of new discoveries.

00:03:47.383 --> 00:03:52.043
More commonly, we'll simply move forward with our research using the information we gained from the gel.

00:03:52.683 --> 00:03:56.373
If we need to isolate specific molecules for further analysis,

00:03:56.383 --> 00:03:57.533
we can do that as well.

00:03:58.643 --> 00:04:00.953
Even though we just scratched the surface in this segment,

00:04:01.272 --> 00:04:06.022
hopefully you now appreciate why gels are such a powerful and integral part of molecular 

00:04:06.022 --> 00:04:07.112
biology research.

